Subject: I**2: "innate" intelligence

On Tue, 6 Apr 1993, Edward J OConnell wrote:
> 
> Gould of course, attacks Social Darwinism, which won't ingratiate him to
> the list. Unextropian as hell. Folks here seem more or less sympathetic to
> the idea that social class, at least in a nominally free market culture,is
> a good indicator of intellectual worth. This is one thing that IQ tests
> have always been good at. Identifying class.  Cyril Burt, the only
> psycholgist ever to have been knighted, used IQ testing to defend class
> differences, in an amazingly unscientific manner. 
> 
> Not only was his methodology for shit, he made up the data. 

This widely-held belief is not yet controversial.  The case against
Burt is made in Hearnshaw's biography, _Cyril Burt: Psychologist_ (1979).
For well-documented strongly dissenting views see R. B. Joynson, _The
Burt Affair_ (1989) and Ronald Fletcher, _Science, Ideology, and the
Media:  The Cyril Burt Scandal_ (1991).  The historical truth of the
matter is obscure, and probably not recoverable, but these authors
make a good case that the case against Burt is nowhere nearly as
indisputable as has been presented in the press, and that Hearnshaw
and other Burt debunkers, many with obvious ideological axes to grind, are
guilty of shoddy scholarship at least.

