<price@price.demon.co.uk> (Michael Clive Price) comments:

> [. . .] Even cretins like me
>who know no foreign languages have heard of Poe's Murders in the Rue
>Morgue?  This question correlated very well with good test performance
>on other questions, according to Ron Hoeflin, the test deviser.

Yes, but this of course is once again merely a test of cultural 
literacy.  It is quite possible to imagine a person of significant
intelligence not knowing of Poe at all; that a person knows of Poe
and his "Murders in the Rue Morgue" would demonstrate a certain
amount of literacy; however, the corollary to this is not necessarily
so.  

Those stupid questions simply demonstrate that merely sharing, as
everyone in a given society is presumed to share certain knowledge,
is no more a useful gauge of intelligence than owning a Mercedes,
or having an open copy of Neuromancer in front of you is
or having access to _any_ set of shared assumptions.

Of course, a similar meme is that the SATs are of use in determining
the intelligence of white, middle-class, Anglo-Saxons.  This is
arguably the case, but if the SATs were a genuine measure of intelligence,
we certainly wouldn't have other tests for foreigners (TOEFL), intelligence
tests like Stanford-Binet and Wechsler, or the literal galaxy of
IQ testers.  I don't believe there's ever been a _conclusive_ study
showing a direct correlation between IQ on any scale and future
earning potential/academic success.

