Date: Tue, 6 Apr 93 14:30:10 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Testing, Verbal Skills, Latin

Regarding the comments about rue:pain::etc. word analogies, the CEEB
(College Entrance Examination Board) once did a study that showed the
taking of Latin in high school correlated strongly with verbal scores on
the SAT. Granted, only the very bright or "nerdish" proto-scholars would
take Latin in high school (and I suspect even fewer high schools offer it
today), and this must be carefully taken into account.

But it seems very reasonable to me that those who study foreign languages,
especially Latin, will be better prepared to answer word analogies of the
type we've been discussing than, say, an equally bright student who
concentrated on something else, even sports (God forbid!).

I did passably well on the Verbal SAT (760...see, I warned you that mention
of tests and scores would result in us gratuitously mentioning our scores!
:-}), but the only person in my school to get an 800 on the Verbal SAT was
a Latin whiz who spent nearly all of her time reading. She was arguably not
as quick-witted as many of the rest of us, but she sure did know those
foreign-derived vocabulary terms!

Likewise, in the Math part, those of us who did a lot of math problems
tended to do pretty well. I was captain of our "Math Team," so math
problems were to me what the Latinate vocabulary terms were to the gal I
just mentioned.

Over the years I've come to better realize the limits of these tests, which
I once considered so important. Those who had very active social lives in
high school but who were otherwise just as bright as the proto-scholar
mentioned above, would naturally not score as highly on a test like the SAT
which emphasizes vocabulary, word analogies, and the whole "mind-set" of
test-taking. 

On a related note, Bill Gates is free to run Microsoft any way he wishes
to, but I find it short-sighted that the main question he used to ask
prospective employees (programmers, presumably) was what their Math SAT and
Advanced Math Achievement Test scores were...those who didn't score 800s
were seen less favorably. (This may be apocryphal.)

Real achievements and sharpness of intellect are more important than scores
on standardized tests. In my opinion.

-Tim May

