From: Michael Clive Price <price@price.demon.co.uk>
Subject: HUMOR: UFO's From Future?

Edgar Swank has reposted a response from Frank Tipler about the
possibilities of time travel:

> [..] as of Feb, '92, current mathematical
> theory is that time travel -is- theoretically possible. 

In 1993 the pendulum seems to be swinging back against time travel - see
"From Wormhole to Time Machine: Remarks on Hawking's Chronology
Protection Conjecture"  Physical Review D, 15 Jan 1993, v47, p554 by
Matt Visser.  I stumbled across the article in the midst of following
up, with Robin Hanson, an article by Tipler: "The Ultimate Fate of Life
in the Universe which undergoes inflation" Phys Lett B v286.  I won't
steal Robin's thunder on the eschatology front (he is preparing
something!), but I feel bound to comment on some of the stuff Tipler
said to Edgar, before commenting on Visser's work:

In his letter Tipler strongly implies that time travel is only
impossible because it violates a *conjecture* called the averaged weak
energy condition (AWEC).  Furthermore he cites
>   1991 Professor Robert Wald of the University of
>   Chicago showed that the postulates of quantum field theory require
>   the validity of the Averaged Weak Energy Condition
but this is not true.  The AWEC, which says that light is never
defocused, has been shown to be false in some types of space-times.  In
particular it is false for cylindrical space-times (which our universe
may well be).  And one disproof is sufficient to show that Prof Wald
must be mistaken, or that Tipler has misunderstood Prof Wald - which to
judge by some of his logic is quite likely.  AWEC has (I think) been
shown to be true on a flat space-time (ie with no gravity) - but that
is hardly applicable to the postulated time-machines which all twist and
distort the continuum.

But if Tipler's logic is flawed, his conclusion - that time travel is
impossible - may well be correct.  This is what Visser shows for a
restricted class of traversable wormholes and conjectures is more
generally true.  That's the bad news.  I hope Edgar doesn't take this
too hard, because there's a sliver lining to the cloud - faster than
light travel is looking much more likely.  The mechanisms that Visser
sketches that block time-travel also remove the paradoxes that forbid
FTL travel.  I'll stop here and save *that* up for another post.

Robin and I will keep you posted.

