Subject: HEX: Reputations and idea futures. 
X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute only with permission

Alexander Chislenko says:
>     Question on price setting:
> 
> If you want to sell 5 Perry positions at 400 Thorns, and I am ready to buy
> 40 at 700 Thorns, we'll have 5 changing hands... but at what price?
> 400? 700? 550?

This usually depends on market rules, but is a rather unusual sort of
thing to happen. Allow me to explain why.

There is, to begin with, a "book" for every security in a market of
outstanding orders. By definition, the book contains only uncrossed
orders. Now, lets say that John Doe has an outstanding ask of 400
thornes by five units, and you want to bid. Assuming that you place a
"buy 5 at market" order, and Doe's ask was at the top of the book, you
take 5 at 400. Assuming that you placed a bid for 40 at 700 (which
never happens -- limit orders like that are never placed), it depends
on the rules of the exchange. Presumably, however, you would get the
top 40 offered on the book assuming they were all offered below 700 --
whether the price would be 700 or the ask price depends on exchange
rules, and is somewhat arbitrary -- given that it never happens, no
one ever worries about it.

> Also, about tying the whole thing to the real values and practical purposes.
> 
> I am buying a new car now - I chose Saturn because of its current reputation
> as the best car in the class.  But Saturns have been on the market for only
> 2 years, and nobody really knows what is going to happen to their reputation
> when the cars get older and start crumbling.
> I might be willing to trade reputation futures/options to hedge myself agains
t
> against too rapid value loss of the car.
> If there are many enough people who believe that the future reputation will
> be high, I'll get cheap insurance (even if they are wrong). Otherwise,
> Saturn, Inc. might want to bet on the cars' reputation - or consider losing
> me as a customer.

The way to do this is to have a futures market in used Saturns. If you
can "pre-sell" your Saturn, you will know for a fact what you can get
for it in two years when you actually want to sell it.

>    Analogously, if I want to appoint Tim May a Secretary of the United Nation
s
> Anarchy Council, I might be interested in estimates of his future reputation
> in the field.

Hard to do that one. With cars, the future price of the car is an easy
measure of value. With people? Much harder.

