Of course humans do alter ecosystems. The question is "is this
important to us all".

The problem I have with the ecofascists is that many of them do NOT
care about humans -- they really honestly would prefer to see us all
die off and leave the earth "pristine" for the animals and plants. It
is impossible to reason with those people and there is no point in
doing so. However, if one's goal is to try to communicate with the
vast bulk of people who's concern for the environment is to see to it
that the planet remains pleasant to live on, well, there it is rather
easy to show that market interests will keep the planet pleasant to
live on. It is harder to show that market forces will preserve all the
territory of the California Gnatcatcher -- but it is also easy to show
that if you are especially concerned for that particular creature the
market gives you a way to preserve it.

