>   I recall reading about how IPD experiments were done where the game was
>   iterated as usual, but the players were rotated at each round (that is,
>   everybody played the same number of rounds, but never against the same
>   person more than once). The results were similar to the normal version.
>
>I'd bet that ALWAYS DEFECT would win strongly, but that the players
>were so socially conditioned that none of them realized the value of
>playing it.  Has anyone replicated this on a computer tournament?


    Interesting coincidance, Dr. Forrest recently discussed an
experament she did with the genetic algorithm evolving
strategies for IPD. Each individuals fitness was determined by
playing against several randomly selected other individuals in
the population each generation. (I think each pair played
several iterations before switching to a new opponant.) The GA
always evolved into a stable tit-for-tat strategy when started
with a random population.
    Always defect was a stable winning strategy, (the only
other stable point found) but the dropoff in fitness for
intermediate strategies was so steep that unless the
population started out with a group very near the stable
point, the whole population evolved toward cooperation.
    Below is an extremely crude two-dimensional ascii graph of
a multidimensional fitness landscape. (Up is more fit, but I
made no attempt at scale in either direction. The horizontal
axis contains multiple variables.)

1 is always cooperate.
2 is cooperate initially with limited revenge.
3 is cooperate initially with unlimited revenge.
4 is alway defect.

